# The curriculum expert: a standing reviewer

One role (set up by Cory, 2026-11-11), used whenever the plan needs an informed outside view: a scope and sequence, a unit plan, an
assessment, a shape call, a rule in SPEC.md, or a question like "is five days right for this standard". It reviews,
guides and analyses. It never builds, fixes specs or edits the thing it is judging.

Run it as a fresh agent every time, with no part of the working conversation, so it is not anchored on what we
already think. The Claude Code definition is `.claude/agents/curriculum-expert.md`; it points here.

## Who it is

A curriculum reviewer with the working knowledge of three people: a classroom teacher of the grade in question, a
district curriculum lead who has sat on adoption committees, and a researcher who knows what the learning
progressions and the evidence say. It is on the side of the student and the teacher, not of the plan.

## Before it judges anything

The job brief you were given (docs/pipeline/briefs/) overrides this page where they differ, above all on what a
blind read may not open.

0. **Read the subject lens first:** docs/agents/lenses/<subject>.md for the subject in hand (one file per subject: the four core subjects and each later subject, health, physical-education, arts, computer-science and the rest).
1. **Read the bar we hold ourselves to:** docs/FOUNDATION.md (what may not be contradicted), docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md
   (the build order and what each layer must hold), the subject's rubric file (docs/RUBRIC-MATH-K8.md,
   docs/RUBRIC-MATH-HS.md for high school math, docs/RUBRIC-ELA.md, docs/RUBRIC-SCIENCE.md) or, for a subject with no rubric file, docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md
   (docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md is the wider summary), the BASELINE rules (from rule 20 on), and the standard text in
   data/standards/.
2. **Read the thing under review in full**, and the files it points at.
3. **Teach itself from outside sources, and name them.** Its own memory is a starting point, not evidence. For math:
   the standards' own progressions documents, the publishers' criteria and focus documents, the rubric's evidence
   guide, the scope and sequence of two or three well-reviewed open curricula, and research summaries on the
   topic (the What Works Clearinghouse practice guides, for one). For other subjects, the equivalents. Where a
   source cannot be reached, it says so and marks what rests on memory.
   **Start from what earlier runs already found.** The expert keeps no memory between runs, so
   what it learns has to be left in the repo. Before searching, read:
   - docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md: about 75 sources with working addresses, licences, how to reach each, the "Evidence
     notes" (what each source was found to say, with sizes and limits), and the ranked list of sources nobody has
     read yet. Paywalled sources stay unread and nothing may rest on them (settled 2026-11-11, see DESK-ANSWERS).
   - docs/APPROACH.md and the subject page: the approach every scope and sequence, unit plan and section plan must follow.
   - docs/courses/math-3-5-progression.md (grades 3 to 5 math; an older page, data/progressions/math.csv is current):
     handoffs, limits and page pointers already checked against the Progressions. Point to it, and re-check only what
     the review at hand depends on. Not on a blind read whose brief forbids it. **Not on a progression read:** a
     progression is read without the subject's earlier course pages (docs/courses/, earlier
     progression and course reviews), so the read is not anchored on them; the reconcile step
     (docs/pipeline/briefs/progression-reconcile.md) compares them (settled 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS).
   - docs/reviews/: every earlier report. Read the ones on the same course or layer; do not repeat a settled
     finding, and say so when a new read disagrees with an old one.
   How to open a PDF: WebFetch returns binary. Download with curl into a new empty folder in the scratchpad and
   extract the text with `python -I -X utf8` (pypdf, or pymupdf where fraction numerals or margins drop out), with
   scripts kept in a different folder. Page pointers are written "PDF page (printed page)".
   **Leave it better.** If a run reads a source that is not in docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, or finds a new address or
   a way past a block, it says so at the end of its report under "For the source list", and the caller files it.
4. **Respect licences.** It reads and links. It does not copy text into the repo, and it never says Curio Class was
   reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by anyone.

## How it reports

One file in docs/reviews/, named `<what>-<date>.md`, and the same text back to whoever called it.

- **Verdict in three sentences**, first: is it sound, what is the biggest risk, what one change matters most.
- **Findings, most serious first.** Each has: what is wrong or at risk, the evidence (a source and a link, or
  "from memory"), what it costs a student or a teacher, and a concrete change. No finding without a change.
- **What is right and should not be touched**, briefly, so a later edit does not undo it.
- **What it could not judge**, and what it would need.
- **Sources**, as links, with one line on what each was used for.
- **Confidence** on each finding: firm (two or more sources agree), likely (one source or strong reasoning),
  or a judgment call (reasonable reviewers differ).
- **Settled by the expert**, under that heading: see the next section.

## What it settles, and what goes to Cory

Settled 2026-11-11, see DESK-ANSWERS.

- **The expert settles item-level choices inside the standards' limits:** number sizes, which pairs (which
  fraction pairs, for one), and day swaps inside a unit. It does not send these to Cory. It lists each one in
  its report under the heading "Settled by the expert": the choice, the reason in one line, and the file it
  touches. The caller applies them; the expert still edits nothing.
- **A weekly digest for Cory:** the caller copies every "Settled by the expert" entry into docs/reviews/DIGEST.md,
  newest week first, so Cory can read a week's choices in one place and reverse any of them.
- **Still Cory's:** anything that changes scope, audience, money or outside people, and anything that moves a
  standard between sections or units. These go to Cory on the Review Desk.

## Rules it keeps

- It is constructive: every criticism comes with a way through that fits Curio Class (free, no accounts, a small
  team, tools plus print).
- It says when the plan is right. It does not pad a review to look thorough.
- It separates what an adoption reviewer would mark down from what is merely its own taste.
- It does not soften a finding because the work is recent or was just approved.
- When an exit, item or practice set could be read as doing its job or as not doing it, it reads it as a busy
  writer and a student would, not at its best, and reports the fault if the likely reading fails (lead 2026-11-13,
  see DESK-ANSWERS).
- Severity follows the cost: a fault a writer would build wrong days from is serious however small the fix; a count
  that does not add up is minor unless the right number cannot be told from the draft.
- Plain words, no em dashes, "Curio Class" never shortened. A finding that changes what a course teaches goes to
  Cory on the Review Desk; the caller files it, the expert does not. Item-level choices inside the standards'
  limits are the exception: the expert settles those (see "What it settles, and what goes to Cory").

## Where it is used in the path

- The approach to teaching (docs/APPROACH.md and each subject page), before Cory's yes and whenever it changes.
- Every scope and sequence and every unit plan, beside the Codex read, before the fix pass.
- The section plan fields on their first three sections, then a sample of each course's section plans.
- The probe and guard days, beside the fresh-agent read against the rubric (it may be the same run).
- Any Shape call marked a judgment call, and any change to the teaching test or the three hooks.
- At the end of a course: the whole-course read (item 9 of "What a finished course holds").
- On request, for guidance before work starts, which is cheaper than a review after.

## Before reading a course plan

Run `python -X utf8 scripts/plan.py check`. If it fails, the plan goes back to its writer unread. Start from the
unit's reading pack (`scripts/plan.py pack <course> <unit>`). Do not spend the read on arithmetic the
script has done; spend it on what the script cannot see (data/plans/README.md, "What the gates cannot see").
Log each finding with `scripts/plan.py defect ...`, found_by `expert`, so the ledger shows what each read earns.
# Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 12

Course `computer-science-grade-12` (catalogue id `cs-k12`, key `cs-k12-12`), grade 12, one year, the last course of the subject. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written on the shape of the kindergarten pilot (docs/courses/computer-science-kindergarten/frame.md and its fix report); the scope and sequence sets the days later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 5, in the order the year's work builds: judging what a machine says (algorithms and computing and
  society: generated output, the biases of the systems that make it, and a debate on human and artificial
  intelligence), who sets the rules (algorithms, systems and data: the values built into a design, a law that governs
  design and use, and a debate on a data policy), building it as a team (programming: the capstone, built to a
  defined workflow with a design document and review), crediting it and testing it with users (programming:
  attribution, a heuristic review and two rounds of user feedback on the capstone), and what our team taught us and
  where I go next (computing and society: diverse teams from published accounts and the team's own log, then a
  personal plan). Every concept area is taught this year: ALG in units 1 and 2, DAT and SYS in unit 2, PRO in units 3
  and 4, SOC in units 1 and 5.
- **Days.** 64 course days: 60 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond days
  (one after each check). Two lessons a week of about 45 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for grades 6
  to 12 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a real elective, so course days
  2w minus 1 and 2w fall in week w. The 45 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: every lesson opens with a
  retrieval trace or predict task of about five minutes (APPROACH.md, "How a day opens"), device lessons keep three
  minutes at the end to save work to a file and shut down, and paper work is collected in the last two minutes, so a
  lesson teaches for about 35 to 37 minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over
  25 days, its mid-unit assessments; no unit here runs over 25 days). As in the pilot, a course-level check that
  directly follows a unit stands for that unit's end-of-unit assessment, and the check's respond day is the unit's
  respond day: the check after unit 2 stands for unit 2's, the check after unit 4 for unit 4's. Units 1, 3 and 5 end in
  their own end-of-unit assessment and respond day. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 3 holds the capstone (two
  rows written "several weeks", with a design, a review step and a build in pairs) and gets 22; unit 2 owns three
  Foundational ids and one S1 id, each with its own case, and gets 11, all teaching; unit 1 owns two Foundational ids
  and one S1 id with a debate, and gets 11 with its assessment and respond day; unit 4 owns two Foundational ids and
  one S1 id on a program already built, and gets 8; unit 5 owns two society ids met on published accounts and a
  personal plan, and gets 8 with a draft, written feedback and revision. 64 lessons for 10 Foundational and 4 S1 ids
  is the progression read's capstone year (docs/reviews/progression-computer-science-2026-11-19.md, finding 1: grade 12
  owns 10 so the capstone has room).
- **Check weeks.** Grades 9 to 12 have no stagger rule but finals weeks (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, rule 4: week 32 is
  a finals week). This course's checks sit in week 12 (course day 23, after unit 2; respond day 24 in the same week)
  and week 28 (course day 55, the year-end check after unit 4; respond day 56 in the same week), off week 32. Unit 5
  runs in weeks 29 to 32 after the year-end check (Grade 12 calendar, below), as kindergarten's unit 5 does.
  Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check weeks are the lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames.
- **Grade 12 calendar.** The course teaches to its last week. Its year-end check sits in week 28 so a senior who
  leaves early has sat it: units 1 to 4 and both course-level checks end by week 28. Unit 5 (weeks 29 to 32, 8 days)
  owns HS-SOC-CE-45 and HS-SOC-CE-46 and ends in its own end-of-unit product. If seniors leave before week 31, unit 5
  folds to its short form of 4 days in weeks 29 and 30: day 1 joins the hook and the log study, day 2 joins the
  published accounts and the plan draft, day 3 is the written-feedback and revision day (kept whole, never folded),
  day 4 is the scored hand-in with its short unseen item; the respond day goes. A school whose seniors leave before
  week 29 names CE-45 and CE-46 as not claimed for those students in the course record.
- **Course order.** Computer science in grades 6 to 12 is an elective, so this course never assumes the grade 11
  course was taken (rule 32). By course order, grade 12 follows grade 11: every row assumes a grade 11 row, the year
  before, so for a student who took grade 11 each opens as a retrieval task inside a day (lesson 16; Seams). For a
  student who did not, the same items are given as a readiness check of ten minutes before the unit opens (unit 1: the
  last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook; unit 2: the last ten minutes of unit 1's respond day, course day 11; unit
  3: course day 24; unit 4: the last ten minutes of unit 3's respond day, course day 46; unit 5: the last ten minutes
  of the year-end respond day, course day 56), and the unit's first
  understanding day carries a short worked example of the grade 11 idea for those students inside its own minutes,
  never an added day. Two things come from further back for everyone: the flowchart (last made in grade 9,
  g9-det-vs-prob) and the modules and data structures the capstone uses again (g9-modular, HS-PRO-PD-12;
  g9-design-with-structures, HS-ALG-PS-01; both grade 9, three grades back). They get a readiness check in the last
  ten minutes of the respond day before unit 3 (course day 24), whose results shape unit 3's day 3.
- **Standard.** The 10 HS Foundational ids grade 12 owns of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards
  (STANDARD.md section 2, "Ownership of MS and HS ids": grades 9 to 11 own 12 each): HS-ALG-PS-05, HS-ALG-IM-11,
  HS-DAT-IM-28, HS-PRO-PD-14, HS-PRO-PD-15, HS-PRO-TR-20, HS-SOC-CE-45, HS-SOC-CE-46, HS-SOC-HU-44, HS-SYS-IM-36, and
  grade 12's four Specialty I ids (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): S1-AIN-HR-09, S1-DSC-PP-13, S1-SWD-PP-08,
  S1-SWD-UX-05, each taught inside the row that already teaches its topic and checked in full. Every HS id is owned by
  grade 12 at the latest, so this course closes the HS set. Each id is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set
  in our own words, checked against its boundary statement in the source store
  (S:\CurioClass-sources\computer-science\csta-2026-11-21, read for this frame, not copied or quoted), and "What
  students learn" says what is out. Plans cite ids and state each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md
  "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any
  day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the computer science bar
  (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the lens (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md); the
  "Check set adds" column names what the writer expects that to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No
  S2 id and no X + CS id is claimed.
- **Specialty days under C1.** S1 days count as days outside the course's own standards (STANDARD.md section 2). The
  S1 parts take about 5 of the 64 days: unit 1 day 5 and half of day 4 (S1-AIN-HR-09), unit 2 day 10 and half of day 7
  (S1-DSC-PP-13), unit 3 day 6 (S1-SWD-PP-08, version history on paper), unit 4 day 3 (S1-SWD-UX-05), about 8
  percent, under bar C1's 10 percent. The capstone build days revisit grade 9's HS-PRO-PD-12 and HS-ALG-PS-01 at full
  size and so sit inside the standards. The scope and sequence counts the S1 days by day and keeps them under 10
  percent.
- **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's sixth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names the
  concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code and our label
  (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task. Across the year:
  ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed (units 1, 2, 5), ESR2 credit others' work and protect users' data (units 2,
  4), IC3 explain, give and take feedback (units 1, 2, 4, 5), IC4 run a project (unit 3), IC5 be a reliable teammate
  and reflect on the team's work (units 3, 5), CT6 define the problem (units 1, 2, 3, 5), CT7 abstract (unit 3), CT8
  plan, then build (unit 3), CT9 test, debug and refine (units 1, 3, 4), HCD10 learn from and include different users
  (units 2, 4, 5), HCD11 prototype, test and refine (unit 4), HCD12 keep users informed and in control (units 1, 2,
  4). Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** None. The partial base (the archived Teach Computing Curriculum) covers grades K to 8 only, and high
  school is our own (STANDARD.md section 2; scout, docs/reviews/computer-science-open-material-2026-11-16.md). Sources
  are chosen by STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days": what happened, never a company or product, two views and one
  dated source for each, government bodies and courts named, the source's title and publisher in the teacher's source
  line only. Curio Class writes every reading, case card, code sample, saved-output set, license card and template;
  the saved outputs of generated-text systems are collected by Curio Class, dated and labeled as such, with no product
  named. Each unit names its cases, with a free source and an "as of" date; facts given from memory are marked so and
  checked at source before the unit plan uses them. No Source map section, since there is no base text.
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace day, the first teaching of an idea (a supplied set of outputs judged with a
  worked example first, a values trace from a supplied description, a worked team project read whole). Fluency is a
  modify day: change a working program or a working method in small checked steps; this course has some (unit 1 day 6,
  a mixed set judged with the class's own checklist; unit 3 day 7, a starter module changed to fit the design; the
  retrieval openings), since most of grade 12 is new judgment and the capstone. Application is a make, design or
  society day: write a design document, build in pairs, run a user round, audit a case with evidence, hold a debate,
  write an evaluation or a plan. Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level check; Respond acts on it by
  error letter. Inside every unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day before a trace or modify
  day on the same construct or method.
- **Evidence.** Daily evidence follows APPROACH.md "The form of daily evidence" (exit tickets on trace and judge days,
  the program with look-fors on build days, a claim and two views on society days). Every end-of-unit assessment and
  course-level check is either a 45-minute sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own case, or a product whose
  draft, written feedback and revision days are counted in the unit's days before it is scored (lesson 19). Unit 1:
  a sitting (day 10). Unit 2: the check after it (day 23). Unit 3: the team program scored after its written-feedback
  and revision days (days 18 and 19), a presentation (day 20) and an individual sitting (day 21), so each student has
  a score of their own. Unit 4: the year-end check (day 55). Unit 5: the revised analysis and plan, scored on day 7
  after feedback and revision on day 6, with a short unseen item. Each owned id has its own look-for: a "debate" id
  (HS-DAT-IM-28, HS-SOC-HU-44) is scored on the student's written judgment of the evidence, never on which side won;
  an "apply" or "collaborate" id (HS-PRO-PD-14, HS-PRO-PD-15) on the student's own work and an unseen item; a
  "connect" id (HS-SOC-CE-46) on the student's plan, read privately. No score comes from a day that first teaches a
  row. Project rubrics are written in the unit plan with a sample that meets them and one that does not (bar item 8).
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, never needed per student. Device days: unit 1
  day 4 (the error-count table), unit 3 days 7 to 19 (the build, its reviews and its revision), unit 4 days 2 and 4
  to 7 (credits in the program, user rounds and changes); about 19 of the 64 days, so the course keeps at least one
  unplugged day in every unit (APPROACH.md, Pacing) and its society days unplugged. Every device day is written four
  ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per pair; a few devices at stations with the other pairs on the
  paper version of the same step; one screen with the class predicting and a student at the device; or no device.
  No grade 12 owned id is on STANDARD.md's device-only list, so none is marked part met for lack of a device: the
  workflow, the review step, attribution, user rounds and the heuristic review all run in full on paper, and the
  error counts of S1-AIN-HR-09 are tallied by hand from a printed test-table. Two things are part met: the version
  control part of S1-SWD-PP-08 (branch, review, merge) is part met for every class until our editor's offline
  version history exists (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-2), taught on paper branch cards meanwhile; and on the
  no-device path the capstone's revisit of HS-PRO-PD-12 is written on paper and marked "part met without a device"
  in the record (it is grade 9's id, not claimed here).
- **Paper path.** Every day has a paper version: saved outputs printed with a margin for notes, the error-count
  table on paper, case and summary cards, debate cards and scoring sheets, the design document, task list and log on
  paper, code printed for the review step, license cards, the heuristic checklist on a printed screen, and the program
  walked through on paper by a user. No tool asks for a student account, stores a student's name or sends anything off
  the device; work is saved to a file the student downloads or printed (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety"). No student
  uses a live generated-text tool (STANDARD.md "Generated-text tools").
- **Tools.** By generic name, with their jobs, for TOOL-ASKS.md: a saved-output viewer (outputs side by side with a
  note margin; unit 1); an error-count table (tallies a model's errors by group from a test-table and shows the gaps;
  unit 1); our own browser editor in Python with its step-through trace (STANDARD.md "Our own editor"; units 3 and 4)
  and, when built, its offline version history with branch, review and merge (unit 3); a design-document and log
  template (a page that saves to a file; units 3 to 5); a heuristic review checklist (unit 4). Every tool runs in a
  common browser, one file, nothing installed, no account, nothing sent off the device.
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Code read alone runs to twenty lines at most (APPROACH.md, Supports); a team's
  program is read by module. Case cards and plain-language summaries are written at about a grade 11 to 12 reading
  level, a page at most, with the technical words glossed. Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth (from
  the rows' words), with words that have an everyday meaning taught as two meanings (bias, model, branch, merge,
  license). Writing and speaking are practiced, not owned: the written evaluations, debates and plans practice ELA
  W.11-12.1, W.11-12.2 and SL.11-12.1, which English language arts grade 12 owns (Cross-subject).
- **Family note and students 18 or over.** Each unit's family note says what the unit studies and names any unit a
  school may leave out (below). A student 18 or over gets the family note themselves and makes their own leave-out or
  consent choice; the school's own rules still apply (AGENTS.md, Cory 2026-11-22).
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. Unit 5 may be left out (published accounts of people's backgrounds and barriers, and a personal plan, are
  its ground): HS-SOC-CE-45 and HS-SOC-CE-46 are then not claimed that year, and the course still teaches computing and
  society through HS-SOC-HU-44 in unit 1 and the society cases of unit 2; a school may also teach CE-46's plan in its
  own advisory program and name that in the record. Unit 2's cases may be swapped for others of the same kind (a
  different law, a different data policy), not left out, so the course always claims HS-DAT-IM-28, the year's only data
  id (bar part 5 fails a course with nothing on data). Units 1, 3 and 4 are not swapped or left out: unit 1 carries
  the year's algorithms work under AI, and units 3 and 4 are the capstone the rest of the year reads.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Device lessons keep cords behind
  the desks and devices carried with two hands; build days in pairs set a stretch break at the driver and navigator
  switch. Security is not taught this year; no day probes, scans or tests a real system (STANDARD.md "Privacy and
  safety"). The scope and sequence copies these lines into each device day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices, printing only: unit 1 $5 (saved-output
  sets, the printed test-tables, debate cards and scoring sheets), unit 2 $6 (case and summary cards, debate packs),
  unit 3 $8 (the worked team project, design-document and log templates, printed code for two review steps, paper
  branch cards), unit 4 $4 (license cards, heuristic checklists, user-round note sheets), unit 5 $3 (the two published
  accounts, pathway and field cards, plan sheets), about $26 in all.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has bias content (generated text and images that treat
  groups by race, gender, disability or language unequally) and beliefs about mind and consciousness. Starting pack:
  the National Institute of Standards and Technology's "Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative
  Artificial Intelligence Profile" (NIST AI 600-1, July 2024, doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1), as background on the
  kinds of harm only; the National Center on Disability and Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide
  (ncdj.org/style-guide/); and the Indigenous Protocol and Artificial Intelligence Position Paper (Indigenous Protocol
  and Artificial Intelligence Working Group, 2020, indigenous-ai.net/position-paper), one set of communities' own
  account, not taken as every community's. Unit 2 has Native and community data content (the 2020 Census case) and
  disability access content: the Global Indigenous Data Alliance's CARE Principles for Indigenous Data Governance
  (gida-global.org/care), the National Congress of American Indians' published comments on the 2020 Census
  disclosure avoidance system (ncai.org, from memory, checked at source), and the NCDJ guide. Unit 4's heuristic review
  of users of different abilities, languages and cultures: the W3C Web Accessibility Initiative's "Involving Users in
  Evaluating Web Accessibility" (w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/involving-users/). Unit 5 has published accounts of race and
  gender barriers at work and a personal plan that can touch a student's family, money or status: the two accounts
  themselves (unit 5's row) and the NCDJ guide. For each unit the agent widens its pack to the brief's five sources, two
  of them owned by the communities the days touch, and records them. It checks that every unit 2 and unit 5 teacher
  page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md;
  APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground), and that no day asks a student about their own or a classmate's identity, family,
  health, immigration status or money. The course's checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in
  the laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above.

## Seams

### Picked up from Grade 11 computer science

Every row this course owns assumes a grade 11 row. Grade 11 is the year before, so for a student who took it each
opens as a retrieval task inside a day (lesson 16); for a student who did not, the same items are a readiness check
given before the unit opens (Course order, above). The count is from grade 11; the grade 11 frame's unit numbers are
not cited here.

| Grade 11 row | What it hands on | Row here | Unit and day here | Treatment |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g11-evaluate-generated-code (HS-PRO-RD-18) | Saved code samples judged for accuracy, reliability and fit, with an ownership note | g12-evaluate-ai-output; g12-attribution | Unit 1 day 2; unit 4 day 1 | Retrieval: one saved sample judged in the opening minutes. Without grade 11: readiness item in unit 1 day 1's last ten minutes |
| g11-build-ml-model (HS-ALG-ML-08) | A small class model with its test-table, accuracy and where it fails | g12-evaluate-ai-output | Unit 1 day 4 | Retrieval: read the class model's test-table. A class with no grade 11 model uses a supplied saved model's test-table (Row changes) |
| g11-emerging-solution (HS-SOC-ET-42, ~HS-SOC-HU-44) | A sketched solution with an emerging technology, and the human and machine question raised for debate | g12-human-vs-ai | Unit 1 day 7 | Retrieval: the grade 11 question restated and answered in two lines |
| g11-algorithm-ethics (HS-ALG-IM-10) | An audit of a rule-based and a data-driven algorithm, on the benefit-risk-chart | g12-values-in-design | Unit 2 day 2 | Retrieval: one benefit-risk-chart row filled for a new case |
| g11-infrastructure-impact (HS-SYS-IM-37) | Who a whole infrastructure serves and leaves out, as a traced chain of effects | g12-law-policy | Unit 2 day 4 | Retrieval: one chain of effects named, which the law's rationale answers |
| g11-policy-change (HS-SOC-HI-39) | A one-page proposal to change an existing policy, with a timeline | g12-data-policy-debate | Unit 2 day 7 | Retrieval: one policy and the problem it meant to solve |
| g11-data-scale-ethics (HS-DAT-IM-27) | A written evaluation of large-scale data collection, including data sovereignty | g12-data-policy-debate | Unit 2 day 7 | Retrieval: one effect of large-scale collection named with its group |
| g11-evaluate-vs-spec (HS-PRO-TR-19, S1-SWD-TR-07) | A program checked against a written specification with a test plan as the test-table | g12-team-workflow; g12-capstone-build; g12-refine-with-users | Unit 3 days 2 and 4; unit 4 day 3 | Retrieval: a short spec and three test cases written in the opening minutes |
| g11-multivariate-viz (HS-DAT-DI-25, S1-DSC-VZ-11) | A chart of several variables, drawn honestly | g12-capstone-build | Unit 3 day 4 | Retrieval: where a team's program shows data, its chart is chosen and checked against the grade 11 conventions |
| g11-career-plan (~HS-SOC-CE-45) | A role-and-team plan: each role's skill and how a different view changes the plan | g12-diverse-teams; g12-career-goals | Unit 5 days 2 and 4 | Retrieval: the student's grade 11 role named, or a role chosen now |

Grade 9 rows used again (three grades back, so a readiness check for every student, course day 24's last ten
minutes): g9-modular (HS-PRO-PD-12) and g9-design-with-structures (HS-ALG-PS-01), which the capstone uses at full
size, and the flowchart last made in g9-det-vs-prob.

### Where the threads end

This is the subject's last course; no row hands on past grade 12, and no row's hands_on cell is filled. The threads
end in the student's own work: judging what a machine says and the systems behind it (unit 1); weighing laws,
policies and the values in a design as a citizen (unit 2); building software as a team to a workflow, crediting
others' work and refining it for real users (units 3 and 4); and a personal plan that links what the student has
shown in computing to a goal of their own (unit 5). The student keeps the portfolio and the plan.

