## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Judging what a machine says. *When a machine gives an answer, how do we decide whether to trust it, and who is responsible when it is wrong?* Anchor: two saved answers to the same history question, one right and one confident, fluent and wrong, with a made-up source | 11 | g12-evaluate-ai-output; g12-human-vs-ai | HS-ALG-PS-05; S1-AIN-HR-09; HS-SOC-HU-44 | Concept areas: algorithms and design (machine learning), computing and society (humans and computing). Practices: CT9 (check an output against a second source, find the first thing wrong); ESR1 (who an output harms); CT6 (frame the debate's question); IC3 (argue a given side, take the other side's point); HCD12 (what users are told about a system's limits) | Day 1: the hook and the question; readiness items in the last ten minutes for students without grade 11. Day 2 (understanding): how a generated-text system makes an answer, kept qualitative (it continues text from patterns in its training data and does not check truth); a worked judgment of a saved answer first, then pairs judge saved text answers and saved code samples for accuracy, naming the first thing wrong and how they would check it against a second source (HS-ALG-PS-05). Day 3: saved outputs judged for bias and harm with evidence: a set of saved answers about jobs and places, and a printed set of saved images made from neutral prompts that show some groups less or as stereotypes (HS-ALG-PS-05 covers text, images and code). Day 4 (device or paper): errors counted by group for the class model from grade 11 (or a supplied saved model with its test-table) and for a supplied saved set from a system others made, with the gaps compared (S1-AIN-HR-09, quantitative). Day 5: where each bias and limit comes from, in the training data, in the way the system was built, or in how it is used, for both systems (S1-AIN-HR-09, qualitative). Day 6 (modify): the class writes a short checklist from days 2 to 5 and pairs use it on a new mixed set. Day 7: how human and artificial intelligence differ, on consciousness, agency and who is responsible, from cases already met; two views with a dated source each, laid on the benefit-risk-chart; the claim of a human-level machine is presented as a claim people make, with the evidence each side gives (HS-SOC-HU-44). Day 8: structured debate, sides given, on who is responsible when a generated answer harms someone (the person who uses it, the people who build it, the people who govern it). Day 9: each student writes which point moved them and why, with peer feedback. Out: building, tuning or debugging a model, its mathematics, how the brain makes consciousness, any live generated-text tool, any claim that a machine thinks. End-of-unit (day 10): a sitting with unseen items: a new saved output set judged for accuracy, bias and harm (HS-ALG-PS-05); a new error-by-group table read and two limits traced to their source (S1-AIN-HR-09); a short written argument on a new responsibility case (HS-SOC-HU-44); respond day 11. Tools: the saved-output viewer, the error-count table. Curio Class writes: the saved-output sets (dated, labeled as generated), the supplied saved model and its test-table, the case cards and debate pack, the checklist sample. Cases: NIST AI 600-1 (July 2024) for the kinds of harm; the US Copyright Office's "Copyright and Artificial Intelligence, Part 2: Copyrightability" (January 2025, copyright.gov/ai/) and the court record of Thaler v. Perlmutter (US Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit, March 2025) for human authorship and responsibility, from memory, checked at source | First because it reopens the machine-learning thread of grade 11 (the class model and the generated-code samples) while they are fresh, and because the year's later units lean on its habit of judging a claim against evidence. The debate follows the output days so it argues from cases students have just judged (row note: "from cases already met"). After English language arts grade 12 unit 1 has taught the seminar (Cross-subject) | Candidate: kept, a second responsibility case from a court record on day 8 for a class that finishes early, zero added days | Bias content: outputs that stereotype a group are shown as a fault of a system, never as a fact about people; talk stays on how a system was built, tested and used, never on a classmate. Students are not asked to share their own group. Beliefs about mind and consciousness differ by faith and family: the debate is on responsibility and evidence, sides are given, and no view of the soul or mind is marked right. No student types into a live generated-text tool. Agent reads: sensitivity (starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 2 | Who sets the rules? *Whose values are built into our systems, and do the rules we make for computing and data do what they promise?* Anchor: a school's bus-route program (invented for practice) that saves the most miles, and the one family whose stop moves half a mile further from home | 11 | g12-values-in-design; g12-law-policy; g12-data-policy-debate | HS-ALG-IM-11; HS-SYS-IM-36; HS-DAT-IM-28; S1-DSC-PP-13 | Concept areas: algorithms and design (impacts), systems and security (impacts), data and analysis (impacts). Practices: ESR1 (who a rule or a design helps and harms); ESR2 (protect users' data); HCD10 (users left out by a design); HCD12 (consent: users informed and in control); IC3 (debate and feedback); CT6 (what problem a rule is meant to solve) | Day 1: the hook and the question. Day 2 (understanding): tracing the values in an algorithmic system from a supplied description: what it counts, what it ignores, whom it asks, and which priority wins (efficiency, privacy, fairness, openness), worked on the bus-route program first, then a library hold queue (invented for practice) (HS-ALG-IM-11). Day 3: one paragraph that states the system's values and one that names a value the student would change, with two views; peer feedback. Day 4: a law that governs how systems are built and used, read as a plain-language summary: why it exists, the problem it aims to solve, its intended and unintended effects and its gaps, with two views and a dated source: the class case is the US Department of Justice's 2024 rule on web and app accessibility for state and local governments (ADA Title II, April 2024, ada.gov) (HS-SYS-IM-36). Day 5: technical, legal and social approaches compared for the same problems, in groups: a design rule (two-step sign-in required for sensitive records), a law (the Children's Online Privacy Protection Act and the Federal Trade Commission's rule, amended January 2025, ftc.gov), and a social approach (a public campaign or people changing their own habits) (HS-SYS-IM-36). Day 6: each student writes an evaluation of one law's rationale and effects; peer feedback. Day 7: a data policy and how its efficacy is judged with evidence: the class case is the Census Bureau's disclosure avoidance system for the 2020 Census, a privacy protection whose effect on the counts of small, rural and tribal communities was argued with evidence by the Bureau and by tribal nations (census.gov; NCAI, from memory, checked at source) (HS-DAT-IM-28); the S1 part asks how the data choices widened or narrowed gaps for those communities (S1-DSC-PP-13). Day 8: debate preparation, two prepared sides with dated sources, including a social approach beside the rule. Day 9: structured debate on whether the policy did its job, judged on evidence, not on side. Day 10: each student writes their judgment of the policy's efficacy, names one effect on an under-represented group with its evidence and proposes one way to collect a fairer dataset (S1-DSC-PP-13). Day 11: written feedback on days 3, 6 and 10 returned and the pieces revised (practice, not scored). Out: reading statute or rule text, legal drafting, redesigning code, statistical tests of representation, any company's system. End-of-unit: the course-level check after this unit (day 23) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the bus-route and hold-queue descriptions, the plain-language summaries of the three laws, the Census case pack with two views, the debate pack and scoring sheet | After unit 1 because it widens the question from one machine's output to the values and rules around whole systems, using unit 1's habit of judging claims with evidence; values come first (a design choice), then the law that governs designs, then a data policy, so each step builds on the last. Its three rows assume grade 11's audit, infrastructure, policy and data-ethics rows, all retrieved inside the days. Placed before the capstone so unit 3's design document can record whose values shaped each decision | Candidate: kept, the Section 1201 rulemaking of the US Copyright Office (October 2024, copyright.gov/1201/) as a third law card on day 5 for groups that choose copying, zero added days | Disability access (day 4): talk is about what a design lets a person do; use the wording the sensitivity read settles. Tribal data (day 7): the case is told with tribal nations' own published views and the CARE Principles, as nations with rights over data about them, never as a problem group; the sensitivity agent reads it first. The bus-route family is invented; no student's home, stop or route is used. Data policy days may lead a student to speak of their own data or family: the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps. Agent reads: sensitivity (starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 3 | Build it as a team. *Can a team of four build one working program that meets a real need, and how does a workflow keep the team's work together?* Anchor: a supplied small project whose two halves, built by two pairs who never compared notes, do not fit together | 22 | g12-team-workflow; g12-capstone-build | HS-PRO-PD-15; S1-SWD-PP-08 | Concept area: programming (program development, testing). Practices: IC4 (shared goal, tasks, milestones, a log); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch; a review that signs off or returns); CT6 (the problem, its users and criteria); CT7 (modules and a data structure); CT8 (plan in pseudocode and a flowchart, then code); CT9 (test against the test-table) | Day 1: the hook and the question (the readiness check was given on course day 24). Day 2 (understanding): a finished small team project read whole: its design document, task list, roles and review log; how the weeks were split (plan and design, build in pairs, review, final check) (HS-PRO-PD-15). Day 3: designing from pseudocode and a flowchart, with modules and a data structure (list-diagram), worked on the supplied project; readiness results acted on (the flowchart and modules retaught here for those who need it); the capstone revisits grade 9's HS-PRO-PD-12 (g9-modular) and HS-ALG-PS-01 (g9-design-with-structures) at full size, owned there. Day 4 (design): each team writes its design document: the problem, intended users, criteria, modules in pseudocode, a flowchart of the key part, the data structure, the test-table plan from grade 11, roles and tasks, and whose needs and views shaped each decision (S1-SWD-PP-08). Projects from a supplied menu that holds no personal data: a club sign-up checker, a library hold queue, a tutoring timetable, a recycling sort quiz, a bus-route helper on invented stops. Day 5: the review step, on a supplied submission: read code against the design document and the test-table, then sign off or return it with a reason; a right and a flawed sample. Day 6 (unplugged): version history on paper branch cards: branch, review, resolve a clash between two changes, merge (S1-SWD-PP-08; part met for the version control part, see Devices). Day 7 (modify): each pair changes a supplied starter module to fit its design and tests it. Days 8 to 15 (make): pairs build their modules, driver and navigator switching at the halfway signal, a log entry each day; review steps on days 11 and 15, each a teammate reading code against the design document. Day 16: modules joined and the whole team test-table run. Day 17: the draft program and design document handed in. Day 18: the teacher's written feedback against the rubric returned; teams plan fixes. Day 19: revision and a final test-table run; the program is scored after this day. Day 20: each team presents its program with a trace of its key part, each member presenting their own module. Out: a professional project tool, a version-control server, large projects, any personal data in a program, any live generated-text tool. End-of-unit: the team program scored after days 18 and 19, each member's part read from the log, and day 21, an individual sitting with unseen items: find the workflow fault in a new team's design document and log, trace a short given module, and sign off or return a new submission with a reason (HS-PRO-PD-15; S1-SWD-PP-08's review and inclusive norms); respond day 22. Tools: our own editor (Python, step-through trace), the design-document and log template; the editor's version history when built. Curio Class writes: the worked team project with its documents and log, the starter modules for each menu project with tested solutions, the right and flawed submissions, the branch cards, the rubric with a meeting and a non-meeting sample | After units 1 and 2 because the design document records whose values shaped each decision (unit 2) and the team program is the one units 4 and 5 read. The workflow and the capstone are one unit, since the capstone is the team program the workflow builds (row notes). It runs weeks 13 to 23, mid-year onward, so the build, its review and its revision all end before the year-end check. 22 days for two rows written "several weeks" | Candidate: kept, a fifth menu project chosen by a team with the teacher's yes if it holds no personal data, zero added days | Pair and team roles are assigned and switch, so no student holds the keyboard all period or is left out; a student who uses assistive technology takes both roles with it. Teams are formed by the teacher. No project collects names, photos or data about classmates. A teammate who misses days: the log shows what they did, and the teacher's page gives a catch-up role. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Credit it and test it with users. *Whose work is in our program, and does it work for the people it is meant for?* Anchor: the team's own program, with a borrowed function, a library, an image from the web and a saved generated snippet in it, and no credits | 8 | g12-attribution; g12-refine-with-users | HS-PRO-PD-14; HS-PRO-TR-20; S1-SWD-UX-05 | Concept area: programming (program development, testing and refining). Practices: ESR2 (credit others' work); HCD10 (learn from users outside the team); HCD11 (test and refine for use and access); HCD12 (users informed and in control); CT9 (change one thing, test again); IC3 (give and take feedback) | Day 1 (understanding): attribution on a supplied program first: each library, each piece of others' code, each image or sound and any generated code gets a credits line (creator, source, license) and a note on the permission it needs, from supplied license cards (a permissive license, a share-alike license, all rights reserved, public domain) (HS-PRO-PD-14). Day 2 (device or paper): the team writes the credits section of its own program. Day 3: the intended users and setting stated, then a heuristic review of the team's program against a short list (usable, consistent, accessible, user in control), asking how it serves users of different abilities, languages and cultures; worked first on a supplied screen; the recommendations it yields (S1-SWD-UX-05). Day 4: user round one: two users from another team try the program and say what they see and what confuses them; the team records the testing results. Day 5: the team changes one thing at a time (a help command, plainer instructions, text size and contrast, a fixed error) and re-checks each against the test-table and the values in its design document (HS-PRO-TR-20). Day 6: user round two. Day 7: the last changes re-checked; a refinement record (what users and tests showed, what changed, why it serves the intended users better). Day 8: the final program, credits and refinement record handed in (recorded, not scored); pairs practice on mixed retrieval items from units 1 to 3. Out: copyright law, statute text, a survey, fixing every issue, accessibility work beyond established guidelines (the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines named as the guide teams apply). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 55) stands for it. Tools: our own editor, the heuristic review checklist, the design-document and log template. Curio Class writes: the supplied uncredited program and its key, the license cards, the supplied screen for the heuristic review, the user-round note sheets | After unit 3 because both rows act on the finished team program (row notes); attribution first so the program users try is the credited one, and the heuristic review before the user rounds so the team knows its intended users before it listens to them. Ends in week 27, so the year-end check in week 28 can stand for it | Candidate: kept, an extra user round for a team whose second round still shows a blocking fault, folded into day 7, zero added days | Users of different abilities, languages and cultures are studied through the checklist and the users in the room, never by asking a student about their own disability or home language; a student may offer. Users from another team take part by choice and may decline. Agent reads: sensitivity (the W3C page in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 5 | What our team taught us, and where I go next. *How do different people's views make computing better, and where could what I have learned take me?* Anchor: two entries from the team's own log, the day a teammate's question changed the design | 8 | g12-diverse-teams; g12-career-goals | HS-SOC-CE-45; HS-SOC-CE-46 | Concept area: computing and society (career exploration). Practices: IC5 (reflect on the team's work); HCD10 (different views and users); CT6 (frame a personal goal); IC3 (feedback on a draft); ESR1 (a more inclusive field) | Day 1: the hook and the question. Day 2: the team's log studied for two moments where a different view changed a decision, and what the team would have lost without it, laid on the benefit-risk-chart (evidence from the log, never opinion about people). Day 3: two first-person published accounts by computing professionals from different backgrounds: Grace Hopper's oral history interview in the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History Computer Oral History Collection (1968, americanhistory.si.edu, archives center; from memory, checked at source) and Katherine Johnson's account in NASA's published interviews and biography (nasa.gov); how each used computational thinking at work, met a barrier and opened the field to others; students draft their analysis (HS-SOC-CE-45). Picks checked against the grade 6 to 11 rows, which name neither. Day 4: the student's portfolio read for two skills shown in this course, with evidence, and two computing-related fields researched from supplied field cards built from the Bureau of Labor Statistics' Occupational Outlook Handbook, computer and information technology group (bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/, duties only). Day 5: the plan drafted: a goal, two skills with evidence, one pathway to check (a certificate, an apprenticeship, a two-year or four-year program, military service, work), one next step (HS-SOC-CE-46). Day 6: the teacher's written feedback on the analysis and t