he plan returned; both revised. Out: a final career choice, an internship, an interview with a professional, formal research, comparing schools or pay. End-of-unit (day 7): the revised analysis (HS-SOC-CE-45) and plan (HS-SOC-CE-46) handed in and scored, the plan read privately, with a short unseen item: a new team log excerpt in which students name where a different view changed a decision; respond and close day 8. Short form if seniors leave before week 31: Grade 12 calendar, above. Tools: the design-document and log template (the team's log). Curio Class writes: the reading guides for the two accounts, the field and pathway cards, the plan sheet, the unseen log excerpt | Last because both rows read the year's own work: CE-45's log comes from unit 3's workflow (the row's in-grade order) and CE-46's portfolio from units 1 to 4. Its rows hand on nothing, so it can follow the year-end check in week 28, and its short form keeps both ids met for seniors who leave in weeks 31 and 32 | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a computing professional; the standard asks for published accounts, not interviews, and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | The accounts show race and gender barriers at work: told as the professional tells them, with respect; no student is asked whether they face such barriers. A plan can touch family money, immigration status or a family's expectations: the plan is private by default, shared only at the level of ideas, and no pathway is ranked above another; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps. A student 18 or over makes their own leave-out choice. Agent reads: sensitivity (starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

