ad for sense, and a paragraph of the student's own revised for one effect (L.11-12.3a). The literary argument (process, about 1,500 words): W.11-12.1a to e, claims and counterclaims developed fairly and thoroughly, the strengths and limitations of each for a named audience, the norms of literary criticism; an argument map before drafting; one one-on-one conference with the teacher on the draft. Discussion in the settings SL.11-12.1 names: a teacher-led discussion of the closet scene beside the student-led seminars. | The year's hardest literary reading comes early, while the fall has time for it and before units 3 and 4 build on it (the comic pair, the novel). The evidence, theme and word rows need a long text read whole; Hamlet is the standard's own example. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's evidence and theme work (e11-evidence-uncertain, e11-lit-themes). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Kept: Ophelia songs on 27 inside the whole play, zero added days; second Shakespeare soliloquy with context on existing assessment 35, one existing day. | Suicide is discussed (the soliloquy, Ophelia's death), with murder and grief: a content note first, the school's support contacts given, no student asked to argue for or against self-harm or to write in a character's voice about it. Gertrude and Ophelia read with attention to how the play treats women. Sensitivity read, after the safe-messaging guidance the brief names. |
