 days; whole novel, Prufrock and Harlem cluster plus choice set fill the unit. | 'If We Must Die' answers the racial violence of 1919: give the setting before reading (the row's note). Tom Buchanan's racist speech about a book of 'scientific' racism: read as Fitzgerald's portrait of him, stated plainly, never as true. Myrtle's death and Gatsby's are violent. The novel's antisemitic portrait of Meyer Wolfsheim (chapters 4 and 9) named and read as evidence of the narrator's view; the unit falls after US History II's Holocaust unit (its days 42 to 56) for the chapters that carry it, which come after about day 57 here, and both courses coordinate content-note wording at unit planning. Sensitivity read, after the Black-led and Jewish-led study the brief names, and the choice set's own study. |
