tructure. Plessy and Brown are kept here, read for legal reasoning: US History I (Jim Crow) and US History II (civil rights) meet them as history, and US Government picks another of its four rights pairs in a school that runs this course beside it. Before the research project, so legal sources are open to it. If Government ran in the fall, its courts and rights units (its days 23 to 44) came first. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's advocacy map (e11-info-founding-reasoning). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: further court opinion, zero days; majority/dissent and foundational comparisons fill the unit. | The Plessy majority reasons from racial segregation: read it as the law it was, beside Harlan's dissent and Brown (the row's note). Harlan's dissent also contains a passage about Chinese immigrants: name it. Walker's Appeal calls for resistance to slavery in strong terms; read in its 1829 setting. Mahanoy's speech included profanity: summarized, not printed. Sensitivity read, after the Black-led legal and historical study the brief names. |
