ks days 150 to 156 in two forms for two audiences, serving history's unit 12 where its beyond-classroom audience requirement is also supplied by another class or staff; an internal panel alone leaves that history-specific part unfinished; final paper about day 158. Cut order if seniors leave early: the paper's revision days go first, then, where the course runs alone, the second form of the talk; the missing contrasting-audience evidence then remains unfinished. Where paired with US History II both forms stay, since history scores two forms for two audiences (D4.3). The scope and sequence's ten-fold shorter path keeps both forms; the source list and the draft are never cut. The students' own word list for the project (L.11-12.6, review). The portfolio closes the year: the five process pieces, one rewritten with a new approach. | Last, so the whole year is open to it and it is the band's capstone. Shares its question with US History II's capstone where a school teaches both (the shared brief below): ELA owns the process, citation, essay length and talk. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's research cycle (e11-research). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: externally framed research-question pattern, zero days; the owned question is self-generated and revised by evidence. | The student's question may be hard history or a hard personal topic: the teacher screens each question and source set with the content notes of the matching unit; students choose what to share in talks. Student panels rotate, with the teacher as lead judge; another class or staff attends only where school allows. No outside adult panel or extra adult observer is required. |



